Let $s'_n=\eta_{\ast}^{-1}2^{n}$, and let $\{s_j\}$ be the sequence defined in \eqref{f8.3}.
By \eqref{f8.4}, $s_j\lesssim2^jT_\ast$, and the integral mean value theorem,
there exist $0<\theta<1$ and a sequence $ \left\{\tilde{s}_n \right\}$ so that
$$ \left\|\epsilon \left(\tilde{s}_n \right) \right\|_{L^2_x }\lesssim \eta_{\ast} 2^{-n},\quad\tilde{s}_n\in  \left[\theta s'_n,s'_n \right]. $$
Setting $\tilde{t}_n=s^{-1} \left(\tilde{s}_n \right)$, we can apply a Galilean transform so that
$\xi \left(\tilde{t}_n \right)=0$. Arguing as in \eqref{f10.18} and \eqref{f10.19}, we have
	\begin{equation*}
	\left\| P_{\geq \tilde{r}_{n} + \frac{n}{8}} u(t)  \right\|_{U^2_{\Delta} \left( \left[0,\tilde{t}_n \right] \right)}
 \lesssim_{\eta_{\ast}} 2^{-n},\quad \mbox{ if } n \mbox{ sufficiently large. }
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation}\label{f10.23}
	\sup_{t\in \left[0,\tilde{t}_n \right]}E \left(P_{\leq \tilde{r}_{n} + \frac{n}{4}}  u(t) \right) \lesssim_{\eta_{\ast}} 2^{-\frac{5}{12}n},
\end{equation}
where $\tilde{r}_n=\left\lfloor\frac{1}{3}\log_2\tilde{s}_n\right\rfloor$.
Then as in \eqref{f10.20}, inequalities \eqref{qdz} and \eqref{f10.23} imply  
\begin{equation*} 
	 \left\|\epsilon(0) \right\|^2_{L^2_x }
\leq \sup_{t\in \left[0,\tilde{t}_n \right]} \left\|\epsilon(t) \right\|^2_{L^2_x }
\lesssim_{\tilde{c}} 2^{-\frac{5}{12}n}.
\end{equation*}
Letting $n\nearrow \infty$, we find that  $ \left\|\epsilon(0) \right\|_{L^2_x }=0$, which contradicts \eqref{contra}.
Thus, $u$ must be a soliton of the form \eqref{soliton}.

